---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-attentive';
const title = 'Drip vs Attentive for Shopify SMS Marketing (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip and Attentive for Shopify email and SMS: account access, identity, permissions, event handling, drop workflows and contribution economics.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When does Drip fit?",
    "answer": "Drip can fit an email-led Shopify team that wants coordinated workflows and qualifies for its staged US SMS access. Confirm account approval, SAP eligibility, email-linked phone identity and supported sending scope before designing a text journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "When does Attentive fit?",
    "answer": "Attentive can fit a Shopify team evaluating email and SMS journeys with store events and subscriber synchronization. Confirm purchased channels, sending markets, integration settings and required event access in the actual account."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Shopify synchronization establish permission?",
    "answer": "No. Keep email, promotional SMS and transactional purposes separate. Retain capture source, timestamp, disclosure and revocation evidence; a phone number, order or imported customer record alone does not authorize a promotion."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should purchase exits work?",
    "answer": "Verify order identity and event latency, then cancel queued cart and promotional follow-ups across every sending system. Test delayed events, duplicate delivery and replay after reconnection before releasing a journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should I add Sequenzy?",
    "answer": "Only if a separate lifecycle email workflow fills a demonstrated gap. Drip and Attentive both offer email capabilities; another sender needs explicit journey ownership, permission mapping and shared suppression controls."
  },
  {
    "question": "How do I compare costs and results?",
    "answer": "Obtain account-specific quotes for the intended channels, contacts, segments, carrier charges and services. Compare contribution per eligible customer against a comparable holdout after discounts, returns, goods, fulfillment, messaging and operating costs, counting each cost once."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a><span class="mx-2">/</span><span>Drip vs Attentive</span></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Campaign marketing comparison</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Attentive for Shopify SMS marketing</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Start with the journeys your Shopify team needs to own. Drip offers email-led workflows with staged SMS access; Attentive offers email and SMS programs with Shopify integration. Account eligibility, event quality and permission controls determine which setup can support your calendar.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox title="Conditional fits" picks={[
          { label: 'Email-led coordination', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Consider when existing workflows fit and your account qualifies for the required SMS scope.' },
          { label: 'Store-connected messaging', tool: 'Attentive', reason: 'Consider when the purchased email and SMS capabilities and Shopify settings support your journeys.' },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Confirm the actual sending scope</h2>
        <p>Drip is a plausible fit for a team already using its subscriber data and email workflows. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">current SMS page</a> describes staged access for existing US-based paid customers with Support All People enabled. Phone records require email identity. It describes toll-free, one-way sending without keyword-to-join, short codes, two-way replies or RCS at launch. Confirm access in your account; do not plan an SMS-only audience or reply workflow around it.</p>
        <p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/48924286539661-Quick-Glance-SMS-with-Drip">Drip SMS overview</a> and activation guidance should be checked against your account approval when rollout information differs. An existing email plan does not establish SMS activation or its billed scope.</p>
        <p>Attentive can fit a team evaluating store-connected email and SMS together. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/360041854231-Shopify">Shopify integration guide</a> documents subscriber synchronization, Shopify segments, discounts and transactional journeys. The tag supplies browsing, cart and purchase signals. Confirm installation, event visibility, sending regions, channel entitlements and service scope rather than inferring them from an integration listing.</p>
        <p>Attentive's <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4434114124564-Collect-email-subscribers-from-Shopify">Shopify email collection guide</a> distinguishes collecting subscribers from arranging email sending access. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.attentive.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408058397588-Set-up-marketing-subscriber-collection-for-Shopify">subscriber collection settings</a> vary with account channels. Neither product requires a separate email tool by default.</p>
        <h2>Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Drip</th><th>Attentive</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Conditional fit</td><td>Email-led workflows with approved SMS access</td><td>Shopify-connected email and SMS within purchased scope</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Identity check</td><td>Phone requires email identity for SMS</td><td>Verify customer matching and channel subscriber sync</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Launch check</td><td>Account eligibility and supported sender capabilities</td><td>Channel entitlements, tag and integration settings</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Shared responsibility</td><td>Permission, queued exits and external senders</td><td>Permission, queued exits and external senders</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <h2>Identity, purpose and purchase exits</h2>
        <p>Map Shopify customer and order identifiers to each messaging profile. Test guest checkout, changed email, shared phone and duplicate profiles before relying on lifetime-value segments. Record the event source, occurrence time and processing time; distinguish a product view from a cart update and a paid order from an abandoned checkout.</p>
        <p>Maintain permission by channel and purpose with capture source, timestamp, disclosure version and revocation history. Order notifications do not grant promotional permission. Apply opt-outs to the affected scope and propagate restrictions to every relevant sender; do not silently infer that an SMS opt-out means an email unsubscribe. Preserve broader suppression where the customer's request requires it. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">consent controls</a> and the <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">compliance guide</a> with qualified advice for the intended market.</p>
        <p>Assign a named owner to each journey, its send-time eligibility gate and its exception queue. Before a send, recheck permission, subscription status, recent purchases, holdout membership and inventory. Verify recipient-aware quiet-hour settings for the applicable market rather than relying on the store clock. Test opt-out handling and escalation without assuming a one-way product supports general conversations.</p>
        <p>For cart recovery, an order must cancel queued messages wherever that journey runs. Simulate a purchase after queue entry, a delayed event, duplicate delivery and an integration outage. Reconcile Shopify orders against journey enrollments and sends after reconnection. Replay must preserve original event identity and time, deduplicate processing, respect current suppression and avoid reopening an expired promotion. Give an operator authority to pause sends while discrepancies are resolved.</p>
        <h2>Merchant workflow proposals</h2>
        <p>The following named scenarios retain campaign ideas from this comparison as proposed tests. They are illustrative briefs, with no verified merchant results, revenue figures or demonstrated lift. Choose exposure and evaluation windows from traffic, purchase lag and the decision being tested.</p>
        <h3>Ridge Run Coffee</h3>
        <p>Exclude active coffee subscribers from acquisition flash offers; test engaged non-subscribers separately. Reconcile subscription updates before each send to detect channel collisions.</p>
        <h3>Arena Energy</h3>
        <p>Coordinate a flavor drop with an athlete collaboration and a gym-bag interest segment. Keep any shaker incentive in the designated email journey and use SMS for eligible drop timing; compare margin with and without the incentive.</p>
        <h3>Loom & Frame</h3>
        <p>Disclose the real art edition size and test a verified sell-through milestone against launch timing among repeat collectors. Stop the scarcity branch when inventory no longer supports the claim.</p>
        <h3>Driftwood Surf Co</h3>
        <p>Use email for seasonal surf and swell-forecast content, then test restock alerts to an explicit waitlist against a broader eligible audience. Keep VIP access independent of discount codes.</p>
        <h3>Noir Collective</h3>
        <p>Coordinate a recurring streetwear drop and a VIP early-access window. Test a restrained broadcast cadence against the current cadence, with a shared email and SMS contact budget.</p>
        <h3>SoleDrop ATX</h3>
        <p>Prioritize a sneaker waitlist by requested size and available stock. Release general email access only at a predeclared inventory milestone; check that queued messages cannot promote sold-out sizes.</p>
        <h3>Festival Threads Co</h3>
        <p>Tie tour-merch campaigns to city and venue dates. Verify recipient timezone separately from venue timezone and test a city-specific contact cap before adding reminders.</p>
        <h3>Vinyl Vault Records</h3>
        <p>Disclose pressing quantity and test waitlist or repeat-collector access. Validate the lifetime-value segment against returns-adjusted orders rather than treating an imported value field as authoritative.</p>
        <h3>Bloom & Wick</h3>
        <p>Plan holiday teaser, bundle and shipping-deadline stages. Separate gift intent from self-purchase interest so bundle texts and scent-education email serve distinct purposes.</p>
        <h3>Peak Trail Gear</h3>
        <p>Limit clearance tests to demonstrated bargain interest and retain a comparable full-price loyalist holdout. Check whether extra orders offset lost contribution from unnecessary incentives.</p>
        <h3>Sauce Lab</h3>
        <p>Use batch inventory to gate a launch message and a later scarcity reminder. Test the reminder only while the stock claim remains true and suppress purchasers before release.</p>
        <h3>Ember Ceramics</h3>
        <p>Pair maker-story email with a waitlist access text for a limited kiln batch. Preserve queue order and fulfillment capacity rather than promising every waitlisted person stock.</p>
        <h3>Cipher Audio</h3>
        <p>Sequence product education before a launch message to eligible engaged browsers. Test a no-discount launch against the current offer while excluding purchasers and accounting for high-value returns.</p>
        <h3>Patchwork Denim</h3>
        <p>Match one-of-one listings to stated style preferences. Test tailored drops against a comparable generic-message group; invalidate queued links once the unique item sells.</p>
        <h2>Price the whole operating model</h2>
        <p>Request current quotes for each intended channel: platform and contact tiers, billable SMS segments, carrier fees, sender setup, onboarding, services and any integration work. Check message encoding and concatenation rules with representative copy. Forecast both ordinary and peak drops, including list growth and duplicate contacts. Published entry prices do not establish your contract or entitlement.</p>
        <p>For a proposed experiment, define eligible customers and a comparable holdout before exposure. Compute contribution from net sales after discounts and returns, less goods, fulfillment, payment, messaging and allocated platform and operating costs. Deduct each cost once; do not subtract discounts or refunds again when already included in net sales. Compare contribution per eligible customer between groups, accounting for different observation windows. Platform-attributed revenue alone cannot establish incremental lift. See <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing economics</a>.</p>
        <h2>Choose an owner before adding another sender</h2>
        <p>Choose Drip when its email workflow model and approved SMS scope match the work. Choose Attentive when its entitled channel capabilities and verified Shopify event paths match the program. A constrained release should pass permission, purchase-exit, replay and reconciliation checks before wider exposure; no fixed pilot length proves fit.</p>
        <p>If considering <a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy</a> for a separate lifecycle task, first document a specific gap, an accountable journey owner and suppression propagation between systems. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a> and the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a> as further evaluation material, not evidence of a universal ranking.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Attentive FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
